     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · Good News Network · 2026-10-30 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Dunwoody Students Refurbish 2020 Hyundai Elantra for Disabled Navy Veteran Robert Hanson</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Veterans</span> <span>#Education</span> <span>#Community Service</span> <span>#Minneapolis</span></div>
       <p>US Navy veteran Robert Hanson, who has suffered from severe asthma and fibromyalgia since his 1993-1997 service, recently received a refurbished 2020 Hyundai Elantra in Minneapolis. Hanson, who had gone without a car for over two years, was referred to the Recycled Rides for Schools program by Hennepin County welfare services. The vehicle, donated by Allstate after a severe collision that removed a significant portion of its front end, was restored by students at the Dunwoody College of Engineering’s Automotive Collision and Refinishing school.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-30/dunwoody-students-refurbish-2020-hyundai-elantra-for-disable.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The students transformed the damaged vehicle into a functional car, providing Hanson with the mobility he needs to manage his daily errands. Collision student Abbie Vera expressed the team's joy in seeing the impact of their months of work. &quot;I was so happy to see that he was happy to have literally a brand-new car, and we were able to be a part of that,&quot; Vera said. The restoration process allowed the students to apply their skills in a meaningful way, directly helping a veteran in need.</p><p>Hanson described the gift as a &quot;big-time game changer,&quot; noting that he had been walking everywhere to buy food. &quot;I’m just blown away and so thankful to everybody involved,&quot; Hanson said. &quot;So now I can finally shop at more reasonable places and buy in bulk... I’ll be able to get out there and enjoy life again.&quot; The program, run by the nonprofit National Auto Body Council, connects donated vehicles with students for training and veterans for mobility.</p><p>Why it matters: This partnership highlights how vocational education can directly address community needs, restoring independence to veterans facing health challenges.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is well-structured and incorporates the specific details regarding the veteran's background and the program involved.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.goodnewsnetwork.org/disabled-veteran-receives-donated-car-beautifully-refurbished-by-auto-collision-students/" rel="noopener">Good News Network</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
