     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Fruit Fly Brain Stem Cells Organize DNA via External Nuclear Proteins</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>University of Washington researchers discovered that proteins outside the nucleus coordinate with internal proteins to position centromeres in larval fruit fly brain stem cells. Using machine learning and imaging, the team demonstrated that centromeres typically anchor at the top of the nucleus, a process essential for maintaining the spatial orientation of distant genes.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-30/fruit-fly-brain-stem-cells-organize-dna-via-external-nuclear.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>When centromeres were displaced, researchers observed that specific genes also lost their normal nuclear positions. This study, published in the Journal of Cell Biology, suggests that external protein mechanisms are critical for DNA organization, providing new insights into how neural stem cells are regulated and how the brain develops.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Concise and accurate reporting of the study's findings regarding centromere positioning and DNA organization.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-stem-cells-fruit-fly-brain.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
