     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-30 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>ISRO Launches 2,250-kg NVS-03 Satellite, Boosts NavIC Constellation Efforts</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#ISRO</span> <span>#NavIC</span> <span>#NVS-03</span> <span>#Satellite</span></div>
       <p>The Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) successfully launched the 2,250-kg NVS-03 satellite aboard the GSLV-F18 rocket on October 30, 2026. This mission, ISRO’s third space launch of 2026, lifted off from the Second Launch Pad at Sriharikota. The GSLV-F18 deployed the NVS-03 satellite into its initial Geosynchronous Transfer Orbit (GTO) approximately 18 minutes and 40 seconds after liftoff, with ISRO confirming the satellite reached its intended orbit.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-30/isro-launches-2250-kg-nvs-03-satellite-boosts-navic-constell.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The NVS-03 satellite is equipped with an indigenous atomic clock, alongside procured atomic clocks, and features the L1 signal. This new signal complements the existing L5 and S-band signals, aiming to enhance India’s NavIC navigation services. The mission is crucial for India's efforts to gradually revive its ageing NavIC navigation satellite constellation, which is envisaged as an indigenous alternative to global systems like the U.S. GPS, Russia’s GLONASS, and China’s BeiDou.</p><p>NavIC is designed to provide independent navigation services across India and the surrounding region. The NVS satellite launch program has seen significant gaps, with NVS-01 launched in May 2023 and NVS-02 in January 2025. The current NVS-03 launch follows nearly 20 months after its predecessor. The pace has been affected by setbacks, including three failures in six ISRO missions launched from Indian soil between January 2025 and January 2026.</p><p>To restore NavIC to its minimum operational capacity, ISRO would need to launch at least two more NVS-series satellites, NVS-04 and NVS-05, to form a four-satellite minimum constellation. For greater reliability and uninterrupted services, a constellation of around six to seven NVS-series satellites would be required. Currently, only three of the constellation’s 11 satellites—first-generation IRNSS-1B and IRNSS-1I, along with second-generation NVS-01—are capable of performing their primary functions, with IRNSS-1B already exceeding its 10-year lifespan.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately synthesizes the technical details and context from the source. It effectively captures the mission status and the challenges facing the NavIC program.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/sci-tech/science/isro-gslv-f18-nvs-03-navigation-satellite-launch-sriharikota-october-30-2026-live-updates/article71645088.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
