     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-10-30 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Kerala Man Wins Legal Battle Over Re 1 IRCTC Refund, Awarded ₹3,100</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#IRCTC</span> <span>#Consumer Rights</span> <span>#Refund</span> <span>#Legal Battle</span></div>
       <p>A man from Kerala successfully contested a Re 1 shortfall in his train ticket refund before the Kasaragod District Consumer Commission, according to an Indian Express report. The complainant had booked a three-tier AC coach ticket for ₹553 on August 11, 2024, for a journey scheduled on August 20. He cancelled the ticket on August 17, more than two days prior to travel. IRCTC deducted ₹180 as cancellation charges and 5 per cent Goods and Service Tax (GST), amounting to ₹9. While he was due ₹341, he reportedly received only ₹340, leading him to claim the Re 1 shortfall caused him mental agony, confusion, and a breach of trust, demanding ₹2 lakh in damages.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-30/kerala-man-wins-legal-battle-over-re-1-irctc-refund-awarded-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The Commission, led by President Krishnan K and member Beena KG, ruled in favour of the consumer, rejecting IRCTC’s submission as it was filed past the prescribed deadline. The commission explicitly stated that IRCTC made no effort to justify the Re 1 deduction. It ordered IRCTC to refund the missing Re 1 with 9 per cent interest from the cancellation date of August 17, 2024, until realisation. Additionally, the commission directed a payout of ₹100 as compensation for deficiency in service and unfair trade practices, along with ₹3,000 for litigation costs, to be paid within 30 days.</p><p>The ruling emphasized that failing to refund the exact balance constituted a service deficiency and an unfair trade practice, stating, &quot;Just because one rupee is paid less, the said fact really counts.&quot; The commission partly agreed to the complainant's demands, confirming his entitlement to ₹100 as compensation and ₹3,000 for litigation, totalling ₹3,100 plus the Re 1 and interest. This decision underscores that consumers are entitled to precise refunds and compensation for any proven service shortcomings, irrespective of the amount.</p><p>For ordinary savers and taxpayers, this ruling signifies that even minor financial discrepancies in services can be legally challenged and successfully rectified. It reinforces that consumer commissions uphold rights rigorously, holding service providers accountable for accurate transactions and transparent practices. The award of significant compensation and litigation costs for a Re 1 shortfall illustrates that the principle of fair service, not just the monetary value, is paramount, setting a clear precedent for consumer protection.</p><p>What to watch: Future consumer commission rulings on minor refund disputes.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately captures all key details, including the specific refund amounts, interest rates, and the commission's reasoning.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/kerala-man-wins-legal-battle-against-irctc-over-re-1-refund-shortfall-gets-100x-compensation-from-consumer-commission-11793371523265.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
