     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Legality of Appeals Against West Bengal Voter Inclusions Questioned</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Election Commission of India faces scrutiny over appeals filed against the inclusion of 16.10 lakh voters in West Bengal. Critics and opposition leaders argue that the officials who filed these appeals may lack the legal authority to do so under the Representation of the People Act, which designates only the CEO, DEO, ERO, or AERO as authorized regarding electoral rolls.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-30/legality-of-appeals-against-west-bengal-voter-inclusions-que.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Although the ECI stated in a Supreme Court affidavit that the officers acted under court orders, the document did not explicitly confirm the status of the officials as authorized under the Act. Opposition parties have demanded a judicial probe and the postponement of local polls until the re-enrolment drive concludes.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief clearly explains the legal controversy regarding the voter appeals and the relevant authorities under the Act.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/west-bengal/authority-of-officials-who-appealed-against-inclusion-of-names-in-bengal-sir-under-lens/article71645404.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
