     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-10-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Neurodivergent Artists to Lead Workshops at Delhi Diwali Art Mela</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Community</span> <span>#Art</span> <span>#Inclusion</span> <span>#Neurodiversity</span></div>
       <p>Three young neurodivergent artists, aged 14 and 17, are set to lead abstract art workshops for over 50 registered students at the Diwali Art Mela in South Delhi's Gulmohar Park. The event, taking place on October 31 and November 1 from 3 pm to 7 pm, aims to highlight their artistic abilities, creativity, and leadership, moving beyond perceptions of their neurodivergence.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-30/neurodivergent-artists-to-lead-workshops-at-delhi-diwali-art.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The initiative is central to Rashmi Aggarwal's vision, who founded Arts and Dreams in 2024 with her elder son, Lakshya Aggarwal. Rashmi's younger son, Pranav, now 14, started creating art at age six, using it to express his world. Arts and Dreams now supports 55 artists, sharing 20 percent of product profits as royalties, with 20 artists cumulatively receiving Rs 4 lakh. A significant corporate order for 10,000 calendars from a Japanese customer showcased 13 artworks, further amplifying the artists' reach.</p><p>At the mela, Pranav will teach abstract bloom painting, while 17-year-old Aishwarya, who has sold around 500 artworks, will instruct on painting nature. Aarush, 14, who has painted since age three, will guide participants in drawing and painting a boat, inspired by Nainital. The mela, a collaboration between Arts and Dreams, Children in India, and Cure India Foundation, will also feature art-based products, games, and neurodivergent children making chocolates.</p><p>Why it matters: This initiative fosters inclusion and provides neurodivergent artists with platforms for both recognition and sustained earning opportunities.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively synthesizes the background, the specific event details, and the statistics regarding the artists and the initiative.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/changemakers/diwali-mela-neurodivergent-artists-arts-and-dreams-12768463" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       <div class="cityguide"><b>City guide</b><p>More on <a href="https://bharatdn.com/city/delhi/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Delhi</a> - census 2011 population, live weather and air quality, local food and getaways.</p></div>
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-10-30/neurodivergent-artists-to-lead-workshops-at-delhi-diwali-art" data-title="Neurodivergent Artists to Lead Workshops at Delhi Diwali Art Mela"><b>Share:</b>
         <a class="sh-wa" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg class="ic" width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M8 1.8A6.2 6.2 0 0 0 2.7 11L1.8 14.2l3.3-.9A6.2 6.2 0 1 0 8 1.8z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>WhatsApp</a> ·
