     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Researchers Use Marker-Assisted Breeding to Combat Rice Bacterial Blight</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers have utilized marker-assisted breeding (MAB) to develop African rice varieties resistant to the destructive bacterial leaf blight. Triggered by Asian strains of Xanthomonas oryzae pv. oryzae (Xoo) introduced to East Africa around 2019, the disease has caused significant yield losses in countries including Tanzania and Madagascar.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-30/researchers-use-marker-assisted-breeding-to-combat-rice-bact.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>While genome editing provides a precise solution, regulatory hurdles led the team to employ MAB, which transfers resistance genes into elite local varieties without modifying the plant's genome. This technique maintains the properties of original crops while offering a faster, more effective path to protecting harvests from the spreading bacterial infection.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively summarizes the research methodology and the specific problem being addressed.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-advance-east-african-rice-harvests.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
