     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · BS Personal Finance · 2026-10-30 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Sebi Fines CDSL ₹1 Crore for Compliance Lapses</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Sebi</span> <span>#CDSL</span> <span>#Penalty</span> <span>#Compliance</span></div>
       <p>Markets regulator Sebi on Friday imposed a penalty of ₹1 crore on Central Depository Services (India) Ltd (CDSL) for multiple compliance lapses identified during an inspection of its operations in FY2023-24. CDSL has been directed to pay the penalty within 45 days, with recovery proceedings initiated if payment is not made. For an ordinary saver, such penalties underscore the regulator's commitment to ensuring the integrity and smooth functioning of systems that hold their investments, highlighting the importance of strict adherence to rules by financial intermediaries.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-30/sebi-fines-cdsl-1-crore-for-compliance-lapses.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Sebi’s 102-page order detailed several violations, including CDSL's failure to communicate reasons for the rejection of pledge requests to the depository participants (DPs) of both the pledgor and pledgee. This lapse directly impacts investors by leaving them in the dark about why their pledged securities requests were denied, potentially hindering their ability to address issues. Additionally, the regulator found non-compliance with prescribed procedures for handling Client Unpaid Securities Accounts (CUSA) and inadequate measures to prevent unauthorised data transmission, both critical for safeguarding investor assets and personal information.</p><p>Further concerns were raised regarding shortcomings in CDSL's disaster recovery drills. For investors, this is a significant finding: it suggests that in the event of a major system disruption, the processes to restore operations and ensure access to their dematerialised securities might not be as robust as required. Such operational deficiencies could lead to delays and uncertainty for individuals managing their investments, emphasizing the need for depositories to maintain impeccable infrastructure to protect client interests.</p><p>What to watch: CDSL's actions to rectify the identified lapses and bolster its compliance framework.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately and comprehensively covers all the regulatory details provided in the source summary.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/markets/news/sebi-imposes-1-crore-fine-on-cdsl-for-multiple-compliance-lapses-126103001384_1.html" rel="noopener">BS Personal Finance</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
