     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Sophia University Researchers Develop Magnetic WaterPuzzle for Chemistry Education</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Sophia University researchers, led by professor Jiro Kondo, have developed 'WaterPuzzle' to help students visualize hydrogen bonding and molecular interactions. The tool uses 3D-printed molecules equipped with neodymium magnets to represent attractive and repulsive forces, allowing for hands-on construction of ice structures and hydrogen-bond networks in the classroom.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-30/sophia-university-researchers-develop-magnetic-waterpuzzle-f.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Detailed in the Journal of Chemical Education, the project aims to make chemistry more intuitive by moving beyond static textbooks. By physically assembling molecules, students can better observe the structural properties of water and understand the fundamental principles governing molecular geometry and biological processes.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief concisely captures the purpose and utility of the WaterPuzzle project.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-waterpuzzle-magnets-hydrogen-bonds-classroom.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
