     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-30 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Study Finds Crop Diversification Improves Yield Stability</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers from the University of São Paulo found that diversifying crops and maintaining soil cover significantly protects agricultural yields from extreme weather. A study conducted in Rio Grande do Sul, Brazil, between 2022 and 2025, showed that farms using cover crops achieved yields up to 76% above the state average.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-30/study-finds-crop-diversification-improves-yield-stability.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>In contrast, traditional farms relying on monocultures suffered from sharp harvest fluctuations during periods of severe drought and flooding. The study concludes that maintaining year-round vegetation cover keeps soil organisms active, optimizes nutrient cycling, and provides greater stability against climate variations.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Covers the study's scope, results, and methodology effectively within the requested constraints.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-crop-diversification-fields-extreme-weather.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
