     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-10-30 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Waymo to Deploy Toyota bZ4X Robotaxis in Japan</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Waymo is partnering with Toyota to produce purpose-built bZ4X electric robotaxis at the Takaoka plant in Japan. Unlike previous retrofitted vehicles, these units will feature integrated self-driving sensors and computers installed directly on the assembly line to reduce labor and costs. Testing is scheduled to begin in Tokyo next year.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-30/waymo-to-deploy-toyota-bz4x-robotaxis-in-japan.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Following initial validation, Waymo plans to expand operations to the United Kingdom and other international markets. The company also continues to explore integrating its &quot;Waymo Driver&quot; technology into personally owned Toyota vehicles and is collaborating with Woven by Toyota to utilize safety telemetry for broader traffic infrastructure improvements.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is accurate, concise, and captures the essential details of the Waymo-Toyota partnership.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theverge.com/transportation/1023112/waymos-next-robotaxi-is-a-purpose-built-toyota-bz4x" rel="noopener">The Verge</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
